explain.text:
Returning `T` made the mean an `int`. A mean is not the element type: return `double` and convert the total first.

```cpp
double mean() const {
    T total{};
    for (const T& x : xs_) total += x;
    return static_cast<double>(total) / xs_.size();
}
```

screens.prose:
A template parameter can be a number instead of a type. `FixedPoint<Places>` stores a value as a whole number of units, $10^{-\text{Places}}$ each, so arithmetic is exact.

```cpp
template <int Places>
class FixedPoint {
    long long units_;   // value × 10^Places
    // ...
};

FixedPoint<2> price;   // pence
FixedPoint<4> rate;    // FX, 4 places
```

A template is a recipe the compiler fills in, once per set of parameters it is used with:

```cpp
template <typename T>            // a type parameter
T sum_of(const std::vector<T>& xs);

template <typename T>            // a class template
class Series { std::vector<T> xs_; };

template <int Places>            // a number parameter
class FixedPoint;
```
`auto x = sum_of(v);` lets the compiler name `x`'s type, and `decltype(e)` names the type of an expression.

